  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Food assistance and Nutrition Sensitive Support for Vulnerable Food Insecure households,  and Pregnant and Breast 
Feeding Women and Girls (PBWG) through appropriate modalities</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">ltpgtA key component of WFP&apos;s sustainability is the continued improvement of its targeting approach and mechanism, specifically aimed at reaching those most severely affected by food insecurity. In 2024, WFP replaced its General Food Assistance (GFA) programme with a more refined and targeted alternative programme. Through this SHF funded project, WFP will regularly support eligible households in Duma subdistrict in Rural Damascus Governorate. Based on the 2024 HNO and Food Security and Livelihoods Assessments (FSA/FSLA) results, Duma is ranked under severity 4 with high prevalence of food and nutrition insecurity rates. WFP aims to provide emergency food assistance and malnutrition prevention to 4,250 severely food insecure people (850 households). Each eligible household will receive monthly cash-based transfers (CBT) of USD 65 per household for a duration of 12 months under the emergency food assistance programme. In the same time period,&#xa0;&#xa0;in addition to emergency food assistance, PBWGs will receive a monthly electronic food voucher worth USD 36 as a nutritional top-up, which can be used to purchase food from WFP contracted retailers from the onset of pregnancy until the child turns two years old. Furthermore, during each e-voucher distribution to PBWGs, health and nutrition sensitization sessions for women and accompanying men will be carried out to raise awareness on the importance of proper nutrition during pregnancy and breastfeeding, as well as appropriate infant and young child feeding (IYCF) practices.ltbrgtlt/pgt</narrative>
  </description>
